    "ocaml-control-flow-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-control-flow-learn",
      "title": "Learn: two ways to choose",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-control-flow"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Distinguish a condition from a pattern, and say which one each form of choice uses.",
        "Trace which branch a match takes for a given value, and say what the compiler reports when a case is missing.",
        "Implement a choice over a number and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/classify/classify.ml before you write anything. The two functions hold the same kind of decision, written two ways.",
        "The first function names the side a number falls on; the second gives the distance from zero. One of them asks a question, and one of them matches a shape."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a match runs every branch and keeps the last answer. A match takes the first branch that fits and stops there.",
        "Thinking a condition and a pattern are two spellings of the same thing. A condition asks about a value, and a pattern says what the value looks like.",
        "Thinking the compiler runs the match to find out which branch is missing. It reads the types and the patterns, not the values."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the core language",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: pattern matching",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/datatypes.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "OCaml chooses in two ways. `if ... then ... else` asks a question about a value and takes one of two answers. `match` takes a value, tries it against a list of patterns, and the first pattern that fits decides. A pattern says what shape the value has; a condition asks a yes or no question about it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/classify/classify.ml:",
          "code": "(* The name of the side the number falls on. *)\nlet sign_of (n : int) : string =\n  failwith \"implement sign_of\"\n\n(* The distance from zero, which is the number without its sign. *)\nlet magnitude (n : int) : int =\n  failwith \"implement magnitude\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A chain of conditions is written `if ... then ... else if ... then ... else`, and the first condition that holds decides. It reads well when there are two answers, and it reads badly when there are four, because the reader has to hold the whole chain in their head to see which branch runs."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A match is written `match value with | pattern -> expression | pattern -> expression`, and the branches are tried in the order they are written. A pattern can also carry a condition, written `| pattern when condition -> expression`, which is a condition inside a branch. The pattern `_` fits anything, and it is the branch that runs when nothing else did."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "if asks a question; match says what shape the value has.",
            "A match takes the first branch that fits and stops.",
            "A guard is a condition inside a branch.",
            "The compiler can tell when a match leaves a case out."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/control-flow   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/control-flow.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/classify/classify.ml and write the body of sign_of so that it names the side a number falls on: positive, negative or zero. Then run dune runtest test/control-flow and read what it said."
        }
      ]
    },
