    "ocaml-errors-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-errors-learn",
      "title": "Learn: two ways to say it went wrong",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-errors"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe what a result is, and what the caller has to do with one.",
        "Explain how a caller catches an exception, and what happens to the code after the call.",
        "Implement a function that answers with a value or with the reason there is none."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/safe_eval/safe_eval.ml before you write anything. The two functions are the two ways of saying it went wrong, one after the other.",
        "The standard library has a type for a value that is either a success or a failure, and its two cases are named Ok and Error."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking an exception has to be caught by the code that raised it. An exception travels up the stack until something catches it, and a function that raises one may never catch it at all.",
        "Thinking a result is an error. A result is a value that says whether there is an answer, and the compiler makes the caller look at it.",
        "Thinking catching an exception stops the program. The code after the call carries on, with the exception handled."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: exceptions",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: error handling",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/err.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "An OCaml function has two ways of saying that it could not do what it was asked. The first is a value: the standard library has a type for a result, and a result is either an Ok holding the answer or an Error holding the reason there is none. The caller receives it like any other value and has to look at which one it got."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The second is an exception. A function raises one and stops; the exception travels up the stack until a caller catches it with `try ... with`. The code after the catch carries on, and the function that raised the exception knows nothing about it. Division by zero is the exception the standard library already provides."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/safe_eval/safe_eval.ml:",
          "code": "(* The value of the expression, or the reason it has none. *)\nlet safe_eval (e : Ast.expr) : (float, string) result =\n  failwith \"implement safe_eval\"\n\n(* The total of the expressions, or the number the caller gave instead. *)\nlet total_or (exprs : Ast.expr list) (fallback : float) : float =\n  failwith \"implement total_or\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The type of safe_eval says both things at once: `(float, string) result` is a result whose success holds a float and whose failure holds a string. The compiler makes the caller deal with it: a function that returns a result cannot be used where a plain value is expected without the caller saying what to do with the failure."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A result is a value: Ok holds the answer, Error holds the reason.",
            "The type of a result says what the success and the failure hold.",
            "An exception travels up the stack until a caller catches it.",
            "try ... with catches; the code after it carries on."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/errors   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/errors.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/safe_eval/safe_eval.ml and write the body of safe_eval so that it gives back the value of an expression, or the reason there is none. Then run dune runtest test/errors and read what it said."
        }
      ]
    },
